// The statement a `sync` block writes after a call whose body ends inside a branch arm -- the case that
// keeps the handover a *pruned* branch makes from being refused along with the one a splice must not
// make.
//
// `pick` is a function whose body is nothing but a branch, and each arm's last statement is an effect
// (`println`), so every statement of the body sits in one of the arms and nothing of it is left outside
// them. The caller writes `let first = pick(1)` and then another statement. That second statement is a
// statement of the caller's block; the last statement of the branch is a statement of an arm -- one the
// other branch does not take. At the moment the arm still *is* an arm, so a judgement that compares the
// two regions says they are two blocks.
//
// It is right to say so for code a splice keeps (`src/runtime/draft.cpp#handChainPlaceTo` called from the
// inline engine), because a statement of another block does not run whenever a statement of that arm runs
// (src/runtime/branch_arms.cpp refuses exactly that graph, docs/technical/
// 17_graph_rewrite_api_contracts.md 4.1, and a sync edge joins one block's statements,
// docs/design/effects/edges.cn.md 4.6). It is wrong for the place a *pruned* branch hands over
// (`src/passes/opt/generic/generic.cpp#pruneBranch`): the branch is being erased, `eraseNode` re-parents
// the arm's regions, and by the time the graph is committed the arm's code is the block's code. Measured:
// with the same judgement applied to both, this program printed three of its four lines under
// `std::macro std::opt std::nvm` -- `pick: not positive` was gone -- which is a wrong answer, not a
// refusal. The judgement now carries that fact as an argument (`GraphDraft::PlaceHandover`), and the four
// numbers below are the lines it pins. `pick(1)` answers 10 and `pick(-1)` answers 20, both by hand.
//
// The other half -- a statement *not* following an arm's chain tail -- has no case on this tree: with the
// guard in place, all 335 cases under `test/cases/opt` and `test/cases/compile/transforms` run through
// `std::macro std::opt std::nvm` and the guard refuses nothing, because the arm tail a splice can end on
// only arises on the tree that carries the chain-place batch (`benchmarks/mlp/model.cml` there stops at
// "breaks the branch-arm invariant: OPER .. (:io/println) reads OPER .. (time:now)"). This case therefore
// pins the half this tree can see. "No case today" is not "no case ever": when that batch reaches a test
// plan, the half gets its own case, pinned the way that program was measured.
//
// What this case does not say. A green suite on this tree -- `8766 / 8722 / 3` with the fix in -- is not
// evidence that the guarding half works on the tree the defect was found on. It says the change broke
// nothing here, and nothing about the program it was written for; a full green reading looks like an
// endorsement and is not one. That evidence is two runs of `benchmarks/mlp/model.cml` on the tree that
// carries the batch, lane-chainplace at 19e6876a6: `exit=1` and the arm-invariant sentence without the
// fix, `exit=0` with `compile_ms=0.5404949188232422` and three `latency_ms` lines with it.

func pick(x: int): int sync {
    if x > 0 then sync {
        'pick: positive'->println
        return 10
    } else sync {
        'pick: not positive'->println
        return 20
    }
}

func main(): int sync {
    let first = pick(1)
    'after {}'->format<first>->println
    let second = pick(-1)
    'after {}'->format<second>->println
    return 0
}